.TH "cbor::structs" "3" "2026-10-31" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH NAME
cbor::structs, cbor::structs<T>::encode, cbor::structs<T>::decode, cbor::describe_members, cbor::member, CBOR_MEMBER \- any CBOR into a C++ struct and back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<cbor/cbor.hpp>\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
static std::expected<std::string, cbor::error> encode(T const &value);

\f[B]template\f[R] <\f[B]class\f[R] Target>
static std::expected<std::size_t, cbor::error> encode(T const &value, Target &&target);
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]template\f[R] <owning_range_of_chars Encoded>
static std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error> decode(Encoded &&encoded);

\f[B]template\f[R] <owning_range_of_chars Encoded>
static std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error> decode(std::shared_ptr<Encoded const> encoded);

\f[B]template\f[R] <owning_range_of_chars Encoded>
static std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error> decode(std::shared_ptr<Encoded const> encoded, T &&target);

\f[B]template\f[R] <borrowed_range_of_chars Encoded>
static std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error> decode(std::shared_ptr<void const> owner, Encoded const &encoded);

\f[B]template\f[R] <borrowed_range_of_chars Encoded>
static std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error> decode(std::shared_ptr<void const> owner, Encoded const &encoded,
                                                       T &&target);
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]template\f[R] <\f[B]class\f[R] T>
\f[B]struct\f[R] describe_members;

\f[B]template\f[R] <\f[B]class\f[R] T>
\f[B]concept\f[R] has_describe_members = \f[B]requires\f[R] { describe_members<T>::members; };
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]template\f[R] <\f[B]class\f[R] P>
\f[B]struct\f[R] member {
    \f[B]static_assert\f[R](std::is_member_object_pointer_v<P>, \(dqcbor::member takes a non\-static data member only.\(dq);
    map_key key;
    P pointer;
};
.EE
.IP
.EX
#define CBOR_MEMBER(T, m) cbor::member{cbor::map_key(std::string_view{#m}), &T::m}
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]template\f[R] <\f[B]class\f[R] T>
\f[B]class\f[R] structs;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]cbor::structs<T>\f[R] writes a C++ value as CBOR and reads it
back, with the types of a member and the description of a class.
.PP
The examples use the types in test/doc_types.hpp, namespace
\f[CR]reference_structs\f[R].
.SS cbor::structs<T>::encode
Writes \f[CR]value\f[R] as one CBOR item in preferred serialization.
A struct is a map, and its keys come in the order of RFC 8949 4.2.1.
The first form returns the bytes in a new \f[CR]std::string\f[R].
.PP
The second form appends to \f[CR]target\f[R] and returns the count of
bytes that it appended.
\f[CR]target\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::string\f[R], a container of
\f[CR]char\f[R], a \f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R] or a writer.
The function writes no shared value (tag 28, tag 29).
.SS cbor::structs<T>::decode
Reads one item into a \f[CR]T\f[R].
The result is a ref that holds the owner of the bytes, so a
\f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] in \f[CR]T\f[R] stays valid.
No form copies the bytes.
The first form moves an rvalue into a new owner; an lvalue does not
compile.
\f[CR]owner\f[R] keeps the bytes of a borrowed \f[CR]encoded\f[R] alive.
A form with \f[CR]target\f[R] reads into the containers of
\f[CR]target\f[R], with their allocator.
The other forms start from \f[CR]T{}\f[R].
.SS cbor::describe_members
A specialization describes how \f[CR]cbor::structs\f[R] and
\f[CR]cbor::schema\f[R] write and read \f[CR]T\f[R].
It holds \f[CR]tag\f[R], a \f[CR]std::optional<cbor::tag>\f[R], and
\f[CR]members\f[R], a \f[CR]std::tuple\f[R] of member entries.
\f[CR]cbor::untagged\f[R] gives a map with no tag.
The specialization stands outside every namespace, after \f[CR]T\f[R].
.PP
A class that is not an aggregate, or that has a base class, needs a
description.
\f[CR]cbor::structs\f[R] then reads the members, the keys and the tag
from the description only.
.SS cbor::member
One entry of describe_members: the key of a member and a pointer to that
member.
The key is a text literal or an integer from \-2\(ha63 to 2\(ha64 \- 1.
The text is the name as it is written.
.SS CBOR_MEMBER
Makes the member entry of \f[CR]T::m\f[R] with the text of the name
\f[CR]m\f[R] as its key.
A type whose name holds a comma needs a type alias.
.SS Member types
\f[CR]T\f[R], and each member of a struct in \f[CR]T\f[R], is one of the
types in this table.
\f[CR]encode\f[R] writes the item of the right column.
\f[CR]decode\f[R] takes only that item, under its own tag.
.PP
